 Traditional CNC motion planning usually follows a serial pipeline in which trajectory smoothing is performed first, followed by feedrate planning along the smoothed path~\cite{Sun2022,Yan2023}. This pipeline can improve the geometric continuity of CAM-generated tool paths, yet once the smoothed trajectory has been fixed, the subsequent feedrate planner can mainly adjust the motion speed along that prescribed trajectory~\cite{Erkorkmaz2001,Lee2011}. It is difficult at this stage to reuse the spatial freedom inside the contour-tolerance band to reorganize the motion in corner or curvature-transition regions~\cite{Sencer2015,Tajima2016}. As a result, geometric smoothness, feedrate efficiency, and velocity-, acceleration-, and jerk-level feasibility are difficult to treat as coupled objectives in high-precision, high-speed machining~\cite{Zhang2021,Sun2022}.
 
 
-The serial pipeline is mainly implemented through local smoothing, global smoothing, and feedrate scheduling. Local smoothing constructs transition curves between adjacent blocks and is suitable for real-time interpolation because the computation is local and the contour error can be bounded explicitly~\cite{Yan2023,Hu2024}. Higher-order B\'ezier, B-spline, or curvature-smooth transitions further improve acceleration or jerk continuity around corners~\cite{Fan2015,Zhang2018}. Global smoothing reconstructs multiple segments over a larger domain and improves continuity for dense short-block paths through curve fitting or global reconstruction~\cite{Song2021,Yang2015}. Feedrate scheduling then assigns the time law on a selected path and enforces velocity, acceleration, jerk, chord-error, or drive constraints during interpolation~\cite{Erkorkmaz2001,Lee2011}. Later NURBS, S-shaped, and adaptive feedrate schemes further improve feasibility under chord-error, axial-drive, and normal-jerk constraints~\cite{Wu2022,Zhang2021}. Look-ahead interpolation uses upcoming path information to adjust feedrate over short-line or spline tool paths~\cite{Tsai2010,Song2020}. These studies form the main technical basis of the conventional serial CNC motion-planning pipeline.
+The serial pipeline is mainly implemented through local smoothing, global smoothing, and feedrate scheduling. Local smoothing constructs transition curves between adjacent blocks and is suitable for real-time interpolation because the computation is local and contour error can be bounded explicitly~\cite{Hu2024}; however, dense short segments and consecutive corners often restrict the available transition length~\cite{Li2020}. Higher-order B\'ezier, B-spline, or curvature-smooth transitions further improve acceleration or jerk continuity around corners~\cite{Fan2015,Zhang2018}. Global smoothing reconstructs multiple segments over a larger domain and improves continuity for dense short-block paths~\cite{Song2021}; however, computation, iteration, local feature preservation, and real-time implementation remain challenging under strict contour-error constraints~\cite{Yang2015}. Feedrate scheduling can enforce velocity, acceleration, and jerk boundaries on a selected path, but it usually depends on a predetermined geometric trajectory~\cite{Zhang2021}. NURBS and S-shaped schemes further improve feasibility under chord-error and axial-drive constraints~\cite{Wu2022}. Look-ahead interpolation uses upcoming path information to adjust feedrate over short-line or spline tool paths~\cite{Tsai2010,Song2020}. These studies form the main technical basis of the conventional serial CNC motion-planning pipeline.
 
 Despite these developments, the serial pipeline separates decisions that are physically coupled during interpolation. Local and global smoothing first determine the geometric curve available inside the contour-tolerance band, including the corner-transition shape and curvature distribution. Feedrate scheduling then assigns speed to this selected curve, so the later stage can reduce or raise feedrate but has little opportunity to revise the geometric use of the tolerance band. This separation becomes restrictive when short segments, consecutive corners, or rapidly varying curvature appear in the same CAM path~\cite{Li2020,Zhao2013}. A conservative smoothing result can force unnecessary deceleration; an aggressive transition can then require speed reduction to satisfy acceleration or jerk limits~\cite{Sencer2015,Tajima2016}. As a result, geometric smoothness, feedrate efficiency, and velocity-, acceleration-, and jerk-level feasibility are difficult to optimize as coupled objectives under a fixed serial procedure~\cite{Zhang2021,Sun2022}.
 
